<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  R/D-step optimizer budget for the rh_*_sweep.sh paper runs.

  Layered on top of the driver's built-in defaults and the case file's id=4
  via --options, which has per-key precedence, so these two keys are the
  only thing this file changes.

  Why a budget is needed at all: the R step's default budget
  (MaxIterations=10000, OptTol=1e-6) is effectively unbounded on a refined
  mesh. Measured on the surface-paraboloid Poisson case, one R step cost
  2.6 s at 245 DoFs, 11.5 s at 500 DoFs, and had not converged after 1.9 h
  at 1565 DoFs -- the iteration count, not the mesh size, is what runs away.
  Since the sweep applies R at EVERY refinement level (relocation only pays
  when it is re-applied after each refinement), an unbounded R step makes
  the r-carrying arms unrunnable.

  The TOLERANCE is what does the work here, not the iteration cap. On a
  hierarchical mesh at ~1250 DoFs the same step costs 670 s at OptTol=1e-4
  (which never meets the tolerance and ends on the 1000-iteration cap) and
  113 s at 1e-2, which ends on the gradient. The cap is kept only as a
  backstop for the case where even 1e-2 is not reached.

  What the budget costs: the R step stops at a looser stationarity than the
  driver default, so the relocated mesh is a descent point rather than a
  converged minimiser. Any figure produced with this file is therefore a
  LOWER bound on what r-adaptivity can do. Two independent observations say
  the bound is not tight enough to change the conclusions: on a uniform
  1156-DoF mesh the post-R error was 3.70e-03 at OptTol=1e-3 and 3.80e-03
  at 1e-2 (a 3% spread), and in both cases a converged R step made the
  error WORSE than no relocation at all (1.12e-03).
-->
<xml>
 <OptionList id="0">
  <int label="MaxIterations" desc="R-step optimizer iterations" value="1000"/>
  <real label="OptTol" desc="R-step optimizer gradient tolerance (unified name)" value="1e-02"/>
 </OptionList>
</xml>
